f 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506494 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e38d1eab-b846-57b1-8eca-3e79f6301a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506503 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506503 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506503 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506503 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f81580ec-b17d-59a4-8b79-db833bfb273c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506507 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506507 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506507 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506507 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506507 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd21be66-27be-55af-af71-359acfb6c6dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506516 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}-8x=5.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1724c4ea-89ed-51e2-a2bf-8d02f0f074fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506516 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =5+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506525 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation $3{z}^{2}-12=9z$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2236615a-502f-5e6f-8a3b-d0bb2e05f4c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506525 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506548 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c6b5462-ae7e-592e-8c21-3452425e7810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506548 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-218773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1071 "By completing the square, the equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+8=0$ is equivalent to\n\n$\\,\\,3(x\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f9c9fb5-398b-5a72-983c-e3a77bff1e92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506549 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+12x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12x & =-8 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4x) & =-8 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & -8 \\\\ 3[{(x+2)}^{2}-4] & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+2)}^{2}-3\\cdot 4 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2}-12 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =-8+12 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-218766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $9{x}^{2}-18x=7.$  Write the solutions in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*\n\n$\\,{x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cca814ca-cf90-5add-8be7-53a49b278718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506589 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-18x & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $9$ to both sides and then dividing by $9$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9+9 & =7+9 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
